# Unix shell scripts must always check out with LF, regardless of the
# checking-out machine's own core.autocrlf setting. Without this, a
# Windows dev machine with autocrlf=true silently converts run.sh to
# CRLF on checkout -- which breaks the script's own shebang line on a
# real Mac/Linux machine ("env: bash\r: No such file or directory",
# meaning the script never runs at all). Confirmed live: this exact
# corruption happened on this repo's Windows checkout (2026-09-03) and,
# since this repo commonly lives in a cloud-synced folder mirrored
# across machines rather than checked out fresh via git on each one,
# propagated as raw bytes to a real Mac where the same run.sh failed.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# Windows batch/PowerShell files need the opposite -- see CLAUDE.md's
# note on cmd.exe misparsing a bare-LF .bat file in combination with a
# non-ASCII byte.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
